186045443 true] [17592186577195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577195 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=3 e^{- y},\\qquad 3 x>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{5}{3 x}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577195 1072 #uuid "45c5715f-9b0f-5612-a118-607bd8abc3ff" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577195 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3 x}\\cdot 3 \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv \\frac{5}{3 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}3 e^{- y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3}{e^{\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} &=\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577196 1080 "true" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577196 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577196 1083 #uuid "45dea541-accf-5351-87dd-ce5e1122aee6" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577197 1080 "true" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577197 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577197 1083 #uuid "4c7a9207-df6c-50b8-9322-193b412462e5" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577198 1080 "false" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577198 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577198 1083 #uuid "5cc87408-521e-5ff3-81ad-d12b8336cba2" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577199 1080 "false" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577199 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577199 1083 #uuid "5ea23ce8-56d6-5036-b822-761465ad590a" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577200 1080 "false" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577200 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577200 1083 #uuid "5f89bbee-f608-509b-809c-754a660a177f" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186577188 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577201 1075 #edb/ref 17592186577211 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577201 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577201 1077 "I" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577201 1078 #edb/ref 17592186577211 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577201 1079 #uuid "621ceb6c-b367-53bb-a1d7-504317392b55" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186577204 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186577208 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186577214 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=2 e^{- y},\\qquad 2 x - 3>0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1072 #uuid "628c5962-9540-54e0-a743-71b039507ef9" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577202 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 x - 3}\\cdot 2 \\\\ &=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}$ and $y\\prime=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}2 e^{- y} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2 x - 3} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{e^{\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{2 x - 3} &=\\frac{2}{2 x - 3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(2 x - 3 \\right)}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577203 1080 "false" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577203 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577203 1083 #uuid "66b843bb-147d-5696-b9c6-20fb6aa9e3df" 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577204 1075 #edb/ref 17592186577222 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [17592186577204 1075 #edb/ref 17592186577224 13194139537006 17592186045443 true] [175921865